<p>A fire can upend your life in minutes. Once the fire department leaves, you face debris, smoke odor, water-soaked rooms, and an insurance claim that will shape your recovery. The person at the center of that claim is the <strong>fire insurance adjuster</strong>. Most homeowners and business owners in McAllen do not know there is more than one kind of adjuster, or that they work for different people.</p>

<p>This guide explains what a fire insurance adjuster does, how the insurance company’s adjuster differs from a public adjuster, and what you can do to protect your fire claim no matter who is adjusting it.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-1-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster documenting fire and smoke damage inside a house" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Fire claims involve structure, contents, smoke, and water damage, and each needs to be documented.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What is a fire insurance adjuster?</h2>

<p>A fire insurance adjuster investigates a fire loss, figures out what the policy covers, and calculates what it should cost to repair or replace the damaged property. In Texas, adjusters are licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI). Who hires the adjuster determines whose interests they represent.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Adjuster type</th><th>Hired and paid by</th><th>Represents</th><th>Typical role on a fire claim</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Staff (company) adjuster</td><td>Your insurance company</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Investigates, writes the insurer’s estimate, recommends payment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Independent adjuster</td><td>Your insurer, on contract</td><td>The insurer</td><td>Same as a staff adjuster; common after large losses or catastrophes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public adjuster</td><td>You, the policyholder, by written contract</td><td>The insured only</td><td>Documents the loss, prepares the claim, presents and negotiates it with the insurer</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>None of this means the insurer’s adjuster is acting in bad faith. Many are careful and fair. But their job is to evaluate the claim for the insurance company, often under its estimating guidelines and with a heavy caseload. A public adjuster’s only job is to present your side of the claim as completely as the facts and the policy allow. Texas public adjuster contracts must state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.”</p>

<h2>What a fire insurance adjuster does on a claim</h2>

<h3>Inspect visible and hidden damage</h3>
<p>Flames are only part of a fire loss. Heat can warp framing, roof decking, windows, and plumbing. Smoke gets into wall cavities, attics, ductwork, and insulation. Water and chemicals from firefighting soak flooring, drywall, and cabinets. In the humid Rio Grande Valley (RGV) climate, wet materials can grow mold if they are not dried quickly. A careful inspection covers all of these areas, not just the room where the fire started.</p>

<h3>Review the policy</h3>
<p>Most homeowners policies split a fire loss into several coverages, and each has its own limits and rules:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dwelling (Coverage A)</strong> for the house itself.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures</strong> for detached garages, fences, and sheds.</li>
<li><strong>Personal property (contents)</strong>, often subject to special limits on jewelry, firearms, cash, and collectibles.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of use / additional living expenses (ALE)</strong> while the home is uninhabitable.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law</strong> for code-required upgrades, if included.</li>
</ul>
<p>Commercial policies add business income and extra expense coverage. See our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page if your business was affected.</p>

<h3>Estimate repair and replacement costs</h3>
<p>Insurers and public adjusters commonly use estimating software such as Xactimate to build line-item estimates. Differences often come from scope (what is included), quantities, and pricing, more than from the software itself. A line-item estimate lets you compare one side with the other.</p>

<h3>Inventory contents</h3>
<p>Contents claims are often the most time-consuming part of a fire loss. Every item needs a description, a quantity, an age, and a replacement cost. Under a replacement cost policy, the insurer may first pay actual cash value and then release depreciation once items are replaced. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains how that works.</p>

<h3>Negotiate and resolve the claim</h3>
<p>Once both sides have estimates, differences are worked out through re-inspections, supporting documentation, and negotiation. If the disagreement is about the <em>amount</em> of loss and not coverage, many policies include an appraisal clause. Learn more on our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page.</p>

<h2>Insurer’s adjuster vs. public adjuster: a side-by-side look</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner reviewing a fire damage estimate with a public adjuster" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Comparing estimates line by line shows where the scope or pricing differs.</figcaption></figure>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Insurer’s adjuster</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who is the client?</td><td>The insurance company</td><td>You, the policyholder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who pays?</td><td>The insurer</td><td>You, under a written contract (capped at 10% of the settlement in Texas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Writes an estimate?</td><td>Yes, the insurer’s estimate</td><td>Yes, an independent estimate of your loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prepares your contents inventory?</td><td>Usually reviews what you submit</td><td>Often helps build and price it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can give legal advice?</td><td>No</td><td>No. Public adjusters are not attorneys.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can do your repairs?</td><td>No</td><td>No. Texas conflict-of-interest rules under Chapter 4102 bar this.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How to protect your fire claim, whoever adjusts it</h2>

<p>These steps help on any fire claim, whether you hire help or not:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get safe and secure the property.</strong> Follow the fire department’s guidance on re-entry. Board up openings and tarp the roof to prevent more damage, and keep receipts. Reasonable mitigation is a policy duty.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down your claim number, adjuster names, and every call.</li>
<li><strong>Request a certified copy of your policy</strong>, including endorsements and the declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before cleanup: each room, ceilings, closets, the attic, the HVAC system, and the exterior.</li>
<li><strong>Do not discard damaged contents</strong> until they are documented. Keep samples of flooring, cabinets, or fabrics when practical.</li>
<li><strong>Keep receipts</strong> for hotels, meals above your normal costs, mileage, and emergency purchases for your ALE claim.</li>
<li><strong>Put requests and disagreements in writing</strong>, and ask the insurer to explain any denial or reduction in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Do not sign a release or final settlement</strong> until you understand what it covers and what you may be giving up.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> turns these steps into a printable list, and the <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can help you estimate how deductibles and depreciation affect a payment.</p>

