<h2>Texas claim timelines that apply to fire claims</h2>

<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets timelines for most property claims. Insurers generally must acknowledge the claim, start the investigation, and request needed information within 15 days of notice. They then have 15 business days after receiving all requested items to accept or reject the claim, and they can extend that by up to 45 more days if they notify you. Once a claim is accepted, payment is generally due within 5 business days. Different timeframes apply to eligible surplus lines insurers. Many policies also contain contractual deadlines for filing suit, so read your policy and consult a Texas attorney about legal deadlines. If your claim has stalled, our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page explains your options.</p>

<h2>What to expect when the insurer’s fire adjuster visits</h2>

<p>The first inspection sets the direction of the claim, so it helps to be prepared. The insurer’s adjuster will usually walk the property, take photos and measurements, ask how the fire started, and may ask for documents such as the fire department report, receipts, or a list of damaged contents. On larger or suspicious losses, the insurer may also send a cause-and-origin investigator, an engineer, or a contents specialist. Some insurers request a recorded statement or, under the policy’s conditions, an examination under oath.</p>

<p>A few practical tips:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Be present</strong>, or have someone you trust there, and walk every affected room, including closets, the attic, and the garage.</li>
<li><strong>Point out smoke odor and residue</strong> in rooms the fire did not reach. Damage that is never mentioned is easy to leave out.</li>
<li><strong>Answer factually and do not guess.</strong> If you do not know something, say so and follow up in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Ask what happens next:</strong> when to expect the estimate, whether an advance on ALE or contents is available, and who your contact is.</li>
<li><strong>Request a copy of the estimate</strong> in line-item form so you can compare it with your own documentation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Choosing a public adjuster for a fire claim</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Fire-Insurance-Adjuster-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Licensed public adjuster discussing a fire claim with a McAllen property owner" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Ask about licensing, fire claim experience, and fees before you sign.</figcaption></figure>

<p>If you decide to hire help, check:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>License.</strong> Verify the public adjuster license with TDI. Our firm, [[company]], holds TDI license #[[license]].</li>
<li><strong>Fire experience.</strong> Ask how they handle contents inventories, smoke scope, and ALE.</li>
<li><strong>Fees in writing.</strong> The contract must state the fee. Texas caps it at 10% of the settlement, and you can rescind within 72 hours of signing.</li>
<li><strong>Availability.</strong> Ask whether they can inspect promptly and keep you informed.</li>
<li><strong>Honesty.</strong> Be cautious of anyone who promises a specific payout or pressures you to sign that day.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Local considerations for McAllen property owners</h2>

<p>Fire claims in McAllen and Hidalgo County often involve slab-on-grade homes with stucco or brick veneer exteriors and composition shingle roofs. Smoke can spread through attics and duct systems into rooms the fire never reached. High humidity makes fast drying important after firefighting water. Repairs that require permits go through the City of McAllen Building Permits &amp; Inspections department, and the 2024 ICC codes now apply to permitted work. Whether code upgrades are paid depends on your ordinance or law coverage.</p>

<p>For a deeper look at how a public adjuster handles a full fire loss, including structure, contents, and living expenses, read our companion guide on the <a href="/fire-insurance-public-adjuster/">fire insurance public adjuster in McAllen, TX</a>, or visit our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> service page. If you are unsure whether you need help at all, <a href="/claim-changes-knowing-when-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">knowing when to hire a public adjuster</a> walks through the signs.</p>
